WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.870
So far, we have been talking about
rearrangements in unichromosomal genomes.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:13.100
But of course, mammalian genomes are multi
chromosome, and we now want to look at

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:18.280
more complex scenarios of rearrangements
in multi chromosomal genomes.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.935
You may be surprised that we are moving to
seemingly

00:00:21.935 --> 00:00:25.760
more difficult case without even a clue on
how we would

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.245
address a simpler case of sorting
by reversal

00:00:29.245 --> 00:00:32.268
so in the case of a unichromosomal genome.

00:00:32.268 --> 00:00:35.948
But as you will see it turns out that
rearrangements

00:00:35.948 --> 00:00:42.880
of multi chromosomal genomes are actually
counter-intuitively, a simple problem.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.260
We'll start from some example of
rearrangements in

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.220
human genomes, and we'll talk about
Philadelphia chromosome.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.830
So these are normal

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:59.090
chromosome 9 and 22 in human,
and they have genes called

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:03.970
abl and bcr located at these chromosomes.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:09.200
Now, consider a break in two places on

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.640
each of these chromosomes that
separate each chromosome into two.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.320
This is not unlike the two breaks that
reversals do, but in this case these

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.210
two breaks are applied to different
chromosomes.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.880
And then afterwards consider gluing the
segments in

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.920
a new order resulting in new chimeric
chromosomes.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:38.538
As a result something happen, genes abl
and bcr that were located at different

00:01:38.538 --> 00:01:44.712
chromosomes in normal human genomes are
now located at the same chromosome

00:01:44.712 --> 00:01:50.540
next to each other.
And as a result it destroys the regulation

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:58.110
of these genes and leads to a disease
called Chronic Myeloid Leukemia or CML.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:04.140
It's actually this oncogenic fusion gene is

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:10.110
a hallmark of CML.
So, from a computational perspective

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:16.240
the operation that I just showed you is
called "translocation"

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.630
and it's exchange segments of different
chromosomes between each other.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.640
And, thousands of different
rearrangements, and

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.910
many translocations, are known for
different tumors.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:35.830
Knowledge about these rearrangements is
very important for studies of cancer.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.130
In particular the

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:44.290
abl bcr fusion genes in CML

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:50.680
since biologists knew how it looks like,
it enable development of a very

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:56.184
efficient cancer drug called Glivec.

00:02:56.184 --> 00:03:02.970
And so far I showed you an example of when
a single rearrangement leads to a disease.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:07.690
In reality, for many tumors, the situation is
more complex.

00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:14.014
Here I showed you an image resulting from
a technique called

00:03:14.014 --> 00:03:19.321
chromosome painting and as a result of
this chromosome painting, every

00:03:19.321 --> 00:03:24.280
chromosome in a normal
cell should be painted by

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:29.480
a single color.
There is a unique color for every normal

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:34.070
human chromosome.
However, if you start chromosome painting

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:39.520
on a human genome, and in this case there's
this MCF7, human

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.470
breast cancer cell line that you see a
very colorful pictures.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:48.440
This colorful picture illustrates that
hardly

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:53.210
any chromosome remain intact in MCF7.

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:54.610
Since every chromosome

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:58.760
consists of different pieces, sometimes
two, three, even four

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:03.530
pieces, of different human chromosomes
combined together.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:09.350
And it's a very question to figure out what
sequence of rearrangement

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:15.341
has produced the MCF7 Tumor Genes.

00:04:15.341 --> 00:04:20.000
And in the next section we will look

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.790
at operation on multichromosomal genomes
that includes

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:29.990
not only translocations, but also fusions
and fissions.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:33.820
For example, fusion simply combine to
chromosomes and glues

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:38.070
them together and fusion breaks a
chromosome into two.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:42.050
For example, humans have 23 chromosomes,

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.490
but chimpanzee, orangutan
and gorilla have 24

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:50.270
chromosomes because a few million years
ago two

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:54.965
chromosomes in a human chimpanzee

00:04:54.965 --> 00:05:00.580
ancestors were fused together into a
single chromosome

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:05.770
in human.
And in the next section, we will look at

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:10.710
the algorithmic problem that is called the
2-Break Distance

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.080
to address their question of how

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.597
to find rearrangements in
multichromosomal genomes.

